            <ArticleTitle>[Percutaneous interspinous distraction for the treatment of dynamic lumbar spinal stenosis and low back pain].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>495-511</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00064-010-1004-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">SURGICAL GOAL: Surgical treatment of dynamic lumbar spinal stenosis and discogenic/arthrogenic low back pain with a new percutaneous interspinous spacer as a therapeutic alternative to more invasive standard procedures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INDICATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Central, lateral and foraminal dynamic lumbar spinal stenosis. Discogenic and arthrogenic (facet osteoarthritis) low back pain. Symptomatic, segmental hyperlordosis. Disc degeneration with dynamic (reducible) retrolisthesis. Interspinous pain ('Kissing-Spines').</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTRAINDICATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Osteoporosis. Conus-/Cauda-syndrome. Structural spinal stenosis. Spondylolisthesis (degenerative and/or isthmic). Deformities. Previous posterior operation in index segment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SURGICAL TECHNIQUE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Percutaneous, minimally invasive implantation of an interspinous spacer (InSpace ™, Synthes, Oberdorf, Switzerland).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="POSTOPERATIVE MANAGEMENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">Early unrestricted mobilization.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Good early results (after 2 year follow-up) in 42 patients with 76% subjective patient satisfaction rate. No approach related complications. Avoidance of the more invasive alternative procedure (decompression, fusion, total disc replacement) in 76.2% of the patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
